  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Change The Game Academy Nepal</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nepal Change the Game Academy Consortium (NCC) started its work in Nepal from November 2018. NCC consisted of the consortium of CSRC, ICCO Cooperation, Sahakarmi Samaj and Tewa. NCC was responsible for organizing a series of Change the Game Academy (CtgA) training in Nepal with the support from Wilde Ganzen. In the consortium, ICCO Nepal played a coordinating role to plan, implement and monitor the project. In December, 2018 ICCO Cooperation coordinated the Training of Trainers events for local civil society organizations with the help of Wilde Ganzen, MDF and Change the Game Academy. 12 A level trainers from Consortium participated in the training. Participants from India and Kenya were also there in the training program. The fundraising session was facilitated by Ger Robeling and Manju. Similarly, mobilizing support was facilitated by Ewoud Plate and Susan Njambi Odongo.
After preparing trainers to organize 3 Local Fundraising Training and 1 Mobilizing Support Training, the Nepal team did the contextualization of the training content which includes translation of training manual and literature. Then in 2019 and 2020, 4 classroom trainings were conducted along with refresher courses and several virtual coaching sessions. So far 81 participants are trained in Local Fundraising and 29 have been trained on Mobilization Support.
Apart from classroom training, Wilde Ganzen also supported the consortium during the Covid19 pandemic and helped in doing activities related to strengthening women&apos;s groups and enhancing livelihoods of marginalized communities. ICCO cooperation supported the Badi community in Kailali District, Sahakarmi Samaj supported marginalized communities in Dang and Bardiya. Similarly, CSRC supported farmers and daily wage workers in Mahottari District and Tewa also responded to the community with relief items, WASH activities and grant programs for their grantee partners. Total 1409 households were supported with relief materials.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Objective of Change the Game Academy&apos;s program is to strengthen the capacity of local CSO</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The beneficiary of this project are Local CSO members and training participants.</narrative>
  </description>
